  Sometimes the variable is not s.
  Also it's often called  H(jω)  to make explicit that  σ = 0 .

  The transfer function of a system
  is a mathematical expression which binds any input
  to a deterministic output.

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transfer_function        https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Determinism

  As we've shown:
  for LTI filters its value depends on the frequency.
  But for each partial it's a complex factor,
  constant over time,
  and equal to the ratio of the output over the input:

    Y(s)exp(st)/X(s)exp(st)  =  H(s)

  Back in Chapter I (#03),
  using Euler's formula,
  we've demonstrated that:

  "
    [...] any complex sinusoid in the form:

      ƒ(t)  =  X(s)exp(st)

    can be considered the positive-frequency-component of a real sinusoid with:

      Amplitude:    α  =  2 |X(s)|
          Phase:    φ  =  arg( X(s) )
      Frequency:    ω  =  s/j  rad/s    or    f  =  s/2πj  Hz
    "

  Except it could be the negative component,
  depending on the value of s ——
  —— in which case  φ  =  —arg( X(s) )  rad.
  It's ok, we were young and stupid back then.

  Also this was before we knew Laplace.
  So  s = jω  or  σ = 0.
  But this is also our hypothesis
  when feeding our RC filter with analytic signals.
  So what precedes stands.
  And the transfer function  H(s)
  describes the system's response to signals of constant magnitude and phase.
  Which explains why the equation involving a time-varying complex amplitude
  never provided a fully satisfying result.

  Ok back to business.
  So:
  magnitude is twice the absolute value of the complex amplitude
  and phase is its argument.
  So if  y = Y(s)exp(st) = H(s)X(s)exp(st),
  its magnitude is:

    2|Y(s)|  =  2|H(s)X(s)|  =  |H(s)|·2|X(s)|

  In plain English ——
  —— and THIS IS FUNDAMENTAL ——
  —— the magnitude of the output
  is the real amplitude of the input
  multiplied by the absolute value of the transfer function
  ( |H(s)|  or  |H(jω)| ).

  //  Example:
  ωc = 1  rad/s
  ω  = 2  rad/s
              ωc
  H(s)  =  ————————
            s + ωc

  Then:
                │     ωc    │        |ωc|
    |H(jω)|  =  │ ————————— │  =  ———————————
                │  jω + ωc  │      |jω + ωc|

                                  ┌───────────
                   √(ωc²)         │    ωc²
             =  —————————————  =  │ —————————
                 √(ω² + ωc²)     ╲│  ω² + ωc²

  Some steps are useless right now.
  Except this shows that  |H (jω)|  is the square root of a ratio.
  Hence the notation:

    |H(jω)|²

  which just means: "let's temporarily ignore the square root".
  Don't get confused if you stumble upon it:
  it's precisely meant to reduce distraction.
  /*
    (Actually, squared amplitude is also a proper physical quantity
    when measuring power instead of voltage.)
  */

  So:
                _____    __________
    |H(jω|  =  √ ωc²  / √ ω² + ωc² 
                     __________
            =  ωc / √ ω² + ωc²

  With  ωc = 1  and  ω = 2,
  that's:
                     _________
    |H(2j)|  =  1 / √ 2² + 1² 
            _______          ___
    =  1 / √ 4 + 1   =  1 / √ 5

  So if  x  =  5·cos(2t)

  then:
                             ___
    y  =  5·cos( 2t ± k ) / √ 5
           ___
       =  √ 5  · cos( 2t ± k )

  (where k is the yet undefined phase shift.)

  /*
    More generally:
      If we normalise the input's real amplitude to 1,
      the output's real amplitude is  |H(jω)| .
      Such normalisation reveals the absolute value of the transfer function
      as a linear, frequency-dependant, time-invariant, factor
      applied to the input's magnitude,
      regardless of its actual value.
      We'll wait for the next chapter before properly dealing with it,
      but we're in the frequency domain now.
  */

  Now what about the phase?
  Let's simplify notation:

    y  =  H(s)X(s)exp(st)

  and let φ be y's phase.

  then:
                               ╭                           ╮         //  See Chapter I's bonus section
    φ  =  arg{ H(s)X(s) }  =  M│ arg{ H(s) } + arg{ X(s) } │         //  for  arg(z₁·z₂) .
                               ╰                           ╯         //
            ╭                                                   ╮    //  And next chapter (IV),
            │╭                                             ╮    │    //  for  M(φ)
       =  π ││{ arg( H(s) )/π + arg( X(s) )/π — 1 }  mod —2│ + 1│    //  (Working-class modulus fix),
            │╰                                             ╯    │    //  and unit-π vs. radians.
            ╰                                                   ╯    //

  Again, if we normalise the input's phase to zero,
  the output's phase is  arg( H(s) ) .

  E.g.:  ω = ωc = 1  and  arg( X(s) ) = 0

    arg( H(j) )  =  arg{ 1/( j + 1 ) }

    =  M{ arg(1) — arg( j + 1 ) }

    =  M( 0 — arctan(1) )

    arctan(1) = x    <=>    tan(x) = 1

    <=>    sin(x)/cos(x)  =  1

    <=>    sin(x)  =  cos(x)

    <=>    isosceles right triangle (NE or SW)

     =>    |x|  =  ( π — π/2 )/2    =>    ± π/4

    1 > 0    =>    arctan(1) > 0

     =>    x > 0    //  (because odd + strictly increasing)

    —π/2  <  arctan  <  π/2    =>    0 < x < π/2

     =>    x  =  + π/4

     =>    arg(j + 1)  =  π/4

    <=>    arg( 1 / (j + 1) )  =  0 — π/4

    <=>    φ  =  —π/4

